WRITE
1. "title": what this document is, in 2-4 words, named for the fix: "Pricing pilot plan", "Evening staffing trial", "Follow-up routine", "First-month plan". Never "Action plan".
2. "summary": one or two sentences — what they will change, for how long, and what decides whether it stays.
3. "weeks": how long the trial or plan runs, {{ weeks }} unless the fix clearly needs a different length (2-12).
4. "monday": the three or four things to do first, in order. Each one a single action someone could do on Monday morning without software, with "why" — one short sentence on why that step comes first or what it protects.
5. "schedule": what happens across the weeks, 3-6 rows, "when" like "Week 1" or "Weeks 2-5".
6. "message": if the change has to be told to customers or staff, write the message itself, ready to send, in the owner's voice, with "to" saying who receives it. Otherwise null.
7. "measures": the 2-4 numbers to track each week that decide whether it worked. Each has an "id" (m1, m2...), a "name", a "unit", which way it should move ("up", "down" or "hold"), and a "baseline" — the value today, ONLY if it is one of their figures above, with its id in "baseline_from". Otherwise baseline null: they measure it in week 1. Never estimate a baseline.
8. "decision_rule": at the end, what result means keep it, and what means stop. Concrete, in their measures.
9. "assumptions": what this plan assumes that nobody has checked yet — usually what we listed as unverified. The trial should settle them.
10. "if_it_fails": one or two sentences on what to do if the result says stop.

Never invent a figure about their business. A number you propose (a new price, a trial length) must read as a proposal. Plain language, their words, no consultant jargon.

